|~ The language: its topic, `::qlang`, whose page is the first screen a
   session reads and whose members are its chapters, and its kinds, each
   with its page and its declaration; the verbs, refusals and members of
   a kind are declared under it, `:number/add`, in the source of that
   kind. ~|

:qlang
  |~~ qlang is a pipeline language over values. A query starts from a subject, the input it is given, and passes it through steps: each step takes the value the step before answered and answers a new one, and the last answer is the answer of the query. Nothing changes in place. Everything the language handles is a value with a literal that prints and reads back as itself, data, code, prose and failures alike, so the language describes itself in its own values: every name has a page, the pages grow from ::qlang, and a query reads them as it reads any data.

      1. The pipe. `a | b` runs b on the value a answered, its subject: `[3 1 2] | sort | first` answers `1`.

      2. Values. Every value has a kind, a tag that `type` answers: `[1 2] | type` answers `::vec`. Numbers, ::number, are finite doubles: `42`, `-1.5`. Strings, ::string: `"text"`. Keywords, ::keyword, are names that stand for themselves: `:name`. Booleans and null: `true`, `false`, `null`. Vectors, ::vec: `[1 2 3]`. Maps, ::map, have keyword keys: `{:a 1 :b 2}`. Sets, ::set, hold no duplicates and keep one order: `#[3 1 3]` answers `#[1 3]`. JSON is a literal too and pastes into a query as it is, its keys read as keywords: `{"a": [1, 2]}` answers `{:a [1 2]}`. A keyword is not its string: `:a | eq "a"` answers `false`, and `keyword` turns either into the other, `:a | keyword` answering `"a"`.

      3. Reading parts. `/k` reads a key, `/0` an element, `/-1` the last, `/a/b/0` a path, `/` the whole value: `{:a {:b [10 20]}} | /a/b/-1` answers `20`. A part that is not there is a failure. `at` reads softly and answers null where the part is missing, and `coalesce` puts a fallback in its place: `{:a 1} | at :b | coalesce 0` answers `0`.

      4. Commands. A command is a word and its modifiers, separated by spaces: `add 1`, `split ","`, `take 2`. The word is found through the kind of the subject: `count` counts the elements of a vector and the characters of a string. Every modifier is computed once, at the call, against the subject of the step, and the word receives the values: `10 | add 3` answers `13`, and `{:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty` answers `12`, both modifiers read from the record in the pipe. A modifier of more than one word stands in parentheses: `[1 2 3] | take (count | sub 1)` answers `[1 2]`.

      5. Code. A quote, `~(…)`, is code, and a quote computes to itself, so the word receives it unrun and runs it where it needs. `map` runs its code on each element, that element its subject: `[1 2 3] | map ~(mul 2)` answers `[2 4 6]`. `filter` keeps the elements its code holds of, `[1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1)` answering `[2 3]`; `sort` ranks by what its code answers, `[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | sort ~(/n)` answering `[{:n 1} {:n 2}]`; `groupBy` files the elements under what its code answers, `["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy ~(/) | map ~(count)` answering `{:b 2 :a 1}`; `reduce` folds the elements into a seed, calling its word with the value so far as the subject and the element as the modifier, `[1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(add)` answering `6`. Several steps stand in one quote, `map ~(/price | mul 2)`. Without the quote the same text is a modifier computed at the call: in `[{:n 1}] | map /n` the `/n` is read from the vector itself, not from each element, and `map` refuses what it is handed, `::vec/map/code/NotQuoteError`.

      6. Building answers. A literal is a step too: it replaces the value in the pipe, and each part inside it is computed against that value, so an answer is reshaped by writing the shape it takes: `{:first "Ann" :age 30} | {:who /first :next (/age | add 1)}` answers `{:who "Ann" :next 31}`, and `{:a 1 :b 2} | [/b /a]` answers `[2 1]`.

      7. Names. A step sees only the value handed to it: after `/qty |` the subject is a number, and the record it came from is gone unless a name keeps it. `:r /` names the value in the pipe `r`, and `:n (…)` names what its group computes; the value in the pipe goes on unchanged, and the bare word reads the name back, `:r` staying a keyword: `{:qty 2 :price 3} | :r / | /qty | mul (r | /price)` answers `6`. A name is declared once in a scope, and the names a group or a quote declares stay in it.

      8. Elements and order. `count`, `sum`, `avg`, `min`, `max`, `first` and `last` read the elements of a vector, a set or a map. A map's elements are its values, the keys travelling with them: `{:a 1 :b 5} | filter ~(gt 2)` answers `{:b 5}`, and `{:a 1 :b 5} | map ~(add 1)` answers `{:a 2 :b 6}`. `keys` answers its keys, and `entries` the pairs `[key value]`, which `tag ::map` builds back into a map. One order ranks every value, so anything sorts: null, then booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, vectors, sets, maps. `desc` turns a key to descend: `sort ~([(/n | desc) /name])` ranks by n descending, then by name.

      9. Conditions. A condition is a boolean, and nothing else is truthy: `filter` and `if` refuse any other value. `if` takes the condition as a modifier and its two branches as code: `5 | if (gt 0) ~("pos") ~("neg")` answers `"pos"`. Conditions of one value hold together by `allOf` and `anyOf`, whose modifiers are computed at the call, every one of them, so a condition that may fail is guarded by a filter before it: `filter ~(/email | ne null) | filter ~(/email | endsWith ".org")`.

      10. Text. A string splits into a vector and joins back, `"a,b,c" | split "," | join "-"` answering `"a-b-c"`; `lines` breaks text into its lines, and `trim`, `lower`, `upper`, `contains`, `startsWith` and `endsWith` read it. A string is never read as a number behind the reader's back: `parseJson` reads one, `"42" | parseJson | add 1` answering `43`.

      11. Failures. A step that fails answers a failure, a value under the tag of what refused: `"x" | add 1 !| type` answers `::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError`. `|` passes a failure by, and `!|` runs its step only on a failure: `"x" | add 1 !| 0` answers `0`. A failure carries its facts and its trail, the steps it passed with their subjects, and prints as an alert: the subject where it arose cut to an excerpt, and each subject it passed an ::elision, whose `:read` continues the query to it, `!| /trail/1/subject`. `!| explain` answers the page of what refused above the failure, the page saying how to repair the call.

      12. Asking. `::vec/sort | doc` answers the page of a word, `::vec | manifest` the words of a kind, and `::vec/sort | laws` the laws of a word by name, each a page and a query that answers true; `x | type | doc` answers the page of the kind of any value. ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) lists the words of vectors. An answer prints whole; `x | elide 4000` bounds it to four thousand characters of its print, each part left out an ::elision whose `:read` continues the query to it.

      13. A task. Over `{"users": [{"name": "Ann", "age": 30, "tags": ["a", "b"]}, {"name": "Bo", "age": 15, "tags": []}]}`, `/users | filter ~(/age | gte 18) | map ~({:name /name :n (/tags | count)})` answers `[{:name "Ann" :n 2}]`. Look at the input before shaping it, `keys`, `/0`, `type`, `count`, and build the query a step at a time, running each.

      14. The chapters, in the order they rest on each other:
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc) values, kinds and tags;
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc) the steps of a query;
      ::link~(::qlang/names | doc) names and their records;
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc) the words that live on kinds;
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc) the failure as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/code | doc) code as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc) the pages and how to move through them;
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc) the tool the language runs in and the budget of an answer.
  ~~|

:qlang/runsAGroup
  |~~ A group runs its steps on the value in the pipe. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | (add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)

:qlang/replacesTheSubjectByALiteral
  |~~ A literal replaces the value in the pipe. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | [1 2] | eq [1 2])

:qlang/readsJson
  |~~ JSON is a literal of the same map. ~~|
  ::law~("{\"a\": 1, \"b\": [true, null]}" | parse | apply / | eq {:a 1 :b [true null]})

:qlang/takesATagNameForAValue
  |~~ A tag name is a value of its own. ~~|
  ::law~([7 8] | ::Box | eq ::Box)

:qlang/reshapesByALiteral
  |~~ A literal reshapes data, its parts read from the value. ~~|
  ::law~({:first "Ann" :age 30} | {:who /first :next (/age | add 1)} | eq {:who "Ann" :next 31})

:qlang/reshapesEachElement
  |~~ Under `map` a literal reshapes each element. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id 1 :tags [:a :b]}] | map ~({:id /id :n (/tags | count)}) | eq [{:id 1 :n 2}])

:qlang/walksAPath
  |~~ A path walks keys and indices, `-1` from the end. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:b [10 20]}} | /a/b/-1 | eq 20)

:qlang/readsAQuotedKey
  |~~ A key with a space is read quoted. ~~|
  ::law~({"first name": "Ann"} | /"first name" | eq "Ann")

:qlang/failsOnAMissingKey
  |~~ A strict read of a missing key fails. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | /b !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:qlang/readsSoftly
  |~~ `at` reads a missing key as null. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at :b | eq null)

:qlang/fallsBack
  |~~ `coalesce` puts a fallback in the place of null. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at :b | coalesce 0 | eq 0)

:qlang/multipliesTwoFields
  |~~ Two fields of one record multiply by `mulOf`. ~~|
  ::law~({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 12)

:qlang/subtractsTwoFields
  |~~ Two fields of one record subtract by `subOf`. ~~|
  ::law~({:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid | eq 7)

:qlang/groupsAPipelineInALiteral
  |~~ A pipeline inside a literal stands in parentheses. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | [(/a | add 1) /a] | eq [2 1])

:qlang/losesTheRecordAfterAStep
  |~~ After `/qty |` a modifier reads the number, not the record. ~~|
  ::law~({:qty 2 :price 3} | /qty | mul /price !| type | eq ::proj/SubjectNotProjectableError)

:qlang/keepsTheRecordByAName
  |~~ `:r /` keeps the record for the steps after. ~~|
  ::law~({:qty 2 :price 3} | :r / | /qty | mul (r | /price) | eq 6)

:qlang/takesCodeAsAQuote
  |~~ Code a verb runs on each element is a quote. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(/age | gte 18) | count | eq 1)

:qlang/quotesOneWord
  |~~ `~name` quotes one word. ~~|
  ::law~("[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | sort ~/n | first" | parse | apply / | eq {:n 1})

:qlang/refusesAValueForCode
  |~~ A value computed at the call is refused where code is wanted. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | filter (gt 1) !| type | eq ::vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError)

:qlang/namesEachKind
  |~~ Every value has a kind, which `type` answers. ~~|
  ::law~([42 "s" :k [1] {:k 1} #[1] true null] | map ~type | eq [::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set ::boolean ::null])

:qlang/splitsAndJoins
  |~~ A string splits into a vector and joins back. ~~|
  ::law~("a,b,c" | split "," | join "-" | eq "a-b-c")

:qlang/cutsAString
  |~~ A string counts, drops and takes by characters. ~~|
  ::law~("db_host" | [count (drop 3) (take 2)] | eq [7 "host" "db"])

:qlang/countsOccurrences
  |~~ `groupBy | map ~count` counts each value. ~~|
  ::law~(["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy | map ~count | eq {:b 2 :a 1})

:qlang/turnsKeysIntoData
  |~~ Entries turn the keys of a map into data. ~~|
  ::law~({:auth {:port 3000}} | entries | map ~(unionOf /1 {:name (/0 | keyword)}) | eq [{:port 3000 :name "auth"}])

:qlang/buildsAMapFromEntries
  |~~ Entries build back into the map. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | entries | tag ::map | eq {:a 1})

:qlang/rounds
  |~~ `round 2` keeps two digits after the point. ~~|
  ::law~(3.14159 | round 2 | eq 3.14)

:qlang/folds
  |~~ `reduce` folds the elements from a seed. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 2] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 6)

:qlang/ordersASet
  |~~ A set stands in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~("#[:z :x :y] | first" | parse | apply / | eq :x)

:qlang/answersASetByDistinct
  |~~ `distinct` answers a set. ~~|
  ::law~([2 3 2 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])

:qlang/answersASetByKeys
  |~~ `keys` answers a set. ~~|
  ::law~({:y 1 :x 2} | keys | eq #[:x :y])

:qlang/reversesASetIntoAVector
  |~~ `reverse` of a set answers a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:z :x :y] | reverse | eq [:z :y :x])

:qlang/readsTheValuesOfAMap
  |~~ A map's elements are its values, its keys travelling with them. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 5} | filter ~(gt 2) | eq {:b 5})

:qlang/branchesOnABoolean
  |~~ `if` branches on a boolean. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("pos") ~("neg") | eq "pos")

:qlang/refusesTruthiness
  |~~ Nothing is truthy: a number is no condition. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no") !| type | eq ::any/if/condition/NotBooleanError)

:qlang/turnsAStringIntoAKeyword
  |~~ `keyword` turns a string into a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~("draft" | keyword | eq :draft)

:qlang/turnsAKeywordIntoAString
  |~~ `keyword` turns a keyword into its string. ~~|
  ::law~(:draft | keyword | eq "draft")

:qlang/printsText
  |~~ `print` answers the text a value reads back from. ~~|
  ::law~(7 | print | eq "7")

:qlang/namesTheValueInThePipe
  |~~ `:n /` names the value in the pipe. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | :n / | [n (n | add 1)] | eq [5 6])

:qlang/declaresANameOnce
  |~~ A name is declared once in a scope. ~~|
  ::law~(:k 1 | :k 2 !| type | eq ::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError)

:qlang/tagsAFailureByWhatRefused
  |~~ The tag of a failure is the address of what refused. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)

:qlang/opensAFailureToItsFacts
  |~~ `!|` opens a failure to its facts. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| /actualType | eq ::string)

:qlang/refusesUnderThePart
  |~~ A part that breaks its invariant refuses under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add "x" !| type | eq ::number/add/addend/NotNumberError)

:qlang/writesAFailure
  |~~ A failure is written as a literal. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:reason :late} !| /reason | eq :late)

:qlang/raisesAMap
  |~~ `raise` builds a failure from a map. ~~|
  ::law~({:reason :late} | raise !| [type /reason] | eq [::error :late])

:qlang/writesAFailureOfItsOwnKind
  |~~ A tag before the bang names the kind of the failure. ~~|
  ::law~(::Late!{:by 3} !| [type /by] | eq [::Late 3])

:qlang/asksWhetherAValueFailed
  |~~ `false !| true` asks whether a value is a failure. ~~|
  ::law~([!{:k 1} 5] | map ~(false !| true) | eq [true false])

:qlang/holdsTheTrail
  |~~ The trail holds the steps a failure skipped. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| /trail/-1/skipped | eq ~(mul 2))

:qlang/holdsItsSteps
  |~~ A quote is the vector of its steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 2)

:qlang/runsAQuote
  |~~ `apply` runs a quote on a subject. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | apply ~(add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)

:qlang/parsesText
  |~~ `parse` turns text into a quote. ~~|
  ::law~("add 1" | parse | eq ~(add 1))

:qlang/declaresAKindOfYourOwn
  |~~ A label takes a word and a part held to its invariant. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A parcel. ~~| | :Box/put ~(within ~(append w)) | :Box/put/w |~~ A weight. ~~| ~(/0) | :Box/put/w/NotNumberError ::holds~(is ::number) | ::Box[1] | [(put 7) (put "x" !| type)] | eq [::Box[1 7] ::Box/put/w/NotNumberError])

:qlang/givesAPartADefault
  |~~ A part read by `at` has a default. ~~|
  ::law~(:number/plus ~(add by) | :number/plus/by ~(at 0 1) | 5 | [plus (plus 2)] | eq [6 7])

:qlang/runsANamedQuote
  |~~ A quote in a bare name runs by `apply`. ~~|
  ::law~(:q ~(add 1) | 5 | apply q | eq 6)

:qlang/readsAStepAsACall
  |~~ The step of `add 1` is a `::call` named `add`. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1) | first | [type /name] | eq [::call :add])

:qlang/readsADoc
  |~~ A doc splits into its prose and the code it holds. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ~(eq 1) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 3)

:qlang/looksAtTheInput
  |~~ The keys of the first element show the shape of the input. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id 1 :tags ["a"]}] | /0 | keys | eq #[:id :tags])

:qlang/opensTheChapters
  |~~ The links of the first page open its chapters. ~~|
  ::law~(::qlang | doc | links | map ~open | count | gt 5)

:builtin
  |~~ The descriptor, a map of fields under this tag. A host's primitive
      is a name under a label whose value is an empty one, `::builtin{}`,
      and a kind a host declares ends with one: it runs the implementation
      the host handed for its name, `::number/add` and `::vec` the
      language's own, while a name written in steps is a quote.
      A descriptor is data and runs nothing by itself: a bare name bound
      to one reads it.
  ~~|

:builtin/isAValueOfItsOwn
  |~~ An empty descriptor is a value like any other. ~~|
  ::law~(::builtin{} | :c / | [1 2] | c | type | eq ::builtin)

:builtin/endsAPrimitive
  |~~ A primitive's value is an empty descriptor. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/add | binding | /value | eq ::builtin{})

:tag
  |~~ The kind of a tag name, `::Kind` taken as a value. A tag is the name
      of a kind, the interpretation of the value it stands on, and the
      model of the language hangs on it: a value carries its kind as the
      tag over it, a name finds its verb through that tag, and the tag
      names the page of the kind, so `type | doc` reads the page of a
      value's kind. Tags stack over any payload and read from the outside
      in; behaviour does not travel with a value, it is found again
      through the tag wherever the value goes. A kind is a label, a name
      declared by its page, and the tag is the address of that name, so a
      page declared as `:d` makes the kind `::d`; a tag no node answers is
      a kind of identity, which declares nothing.
      ::snippet~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type) answers `::Box`, and
      ::snippet~(kinds) lists the kinds the scope reads.
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:tag/isATag
  |~~ A tag name is a value of `::tag`. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box | type | eq ::tag)

:tag/keepsTheInvariantsOfItsLabel
  |~~ A value put under a label keeps the label's invariants. ~~|
  ::law~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| | :Pos/NotPositiveError ::holds~(gt 0) | ::Pos(-3) !| type | eq ::Pos/NotPositiveError)

:tag/buildsByItsConstructor
  |~~ `::tag` over a tag name is that name. ~~|
  ::law~(::tag::Box | eq ::Box)

:tag/namesAPage
  |~~ The tag of a kind names its page. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | type | doc | type | eq ::doc)

:tag/buildsThroughConstruct
  |~~ A label's `construct` builds the payload. ~~|
  ::law~(:d |~~ A d. ~~| | :d/construct ~({:s /}) | ::d(10) | [type payload] | eq [::d {:s 10}])

:tag/namesNothingUndeclared
  |~~ A tag no declaration answers names no record. ~~|
  ::law~(::Unbound[1] | type | binding !| type | eq ::any/binding/UnknownNameError)

:null
  |~~ The kind of null, the absence of a value; a part a call may leave
      out reads null, `~(at 0)`. Its constructor, as every
      constructor of a kind of the core, reads a payload of the kind as
      the value itself. ::link~(::null/at | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:null/isNull
  |~~ Null is of `::null`. ~~|
  ::law~(null | type | eq ::null)

:null/buildsNull
  |~~ The constructor of null answers null. ~~|
  ::law~(::null(null) | eq null)

:boolean
  |~~ The kind of `true` and `false`, the answer of a question about a
      value: `filter`, `every` and `if` take a quote that answers one,
      and `false !| true` after steps answers whether they failed.
      ::snippet~(::boolean | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:boolean/isABoolean
  |~~ True is a boolean. ~~|
  ::law~(true | type | eq ::boolean)

:boolean/buildsABoolean
  |~~ The constructor of a boolean answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(::boolean(false) | eq false)

:number
  |~~ The kind of a number, a finite double: an infinity or NaN is no
      value, so a step whose result leaves the domain answers an error.
      No kind reads text, so `::number(5)` is `5` and `::number"5"` is
      refused; `parseJson` reads a number out of a string. Its literal
      is JSON's, a leading zero allowed, and its precision the double's
      own: a literal past the range is no number the parser reads, an
      integer and a decimal are one kind, rounded as a double rounds, and
      a step whose result leaves the range answers an error. Its words
      add, subtract, multiply, divide, round and compare numbers.
      ::snippet~(::number | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:number/isANumber
  |~~ Five is a number. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | type | eq ::number)

:number/refusesALiteralPastTheRange
  |~~ The literal 1e400 does not parse. ~~|
  ::law~("1e400" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)

:number/answersAnErrorPastTheRange
  |~~ A product past the range answers an error. ~~|
  ::law~(1e308 | mul 10 !| type | eq ::number/NotFiniteError)

:number/roundsAsADouble
  |~~ A tenth and two tenths add as doubles add. ~~|
  ::law~([-1 2.5e-3 (0.1 | add 0.2)] | eq [-1 0.0025 0.30000000000000004])

:string
  |~~ The kind of a string, text as it came. A JSON string arrives as a
      string, and `keyword` makes one a keyword where a key is wanted,
      since the keys of a map are keywords. Its verbs work on the text
      whole, by its characters, `count`, `take` and `drop`, or by its
      lines, and `lines` breaks it into the vector a pipeline reads in
      pieces. Its literal is written in double quotes with the escapes of
      JSON, `\"`, `\\`, `\n`, `\t` and `\uXXXX` among them, any other
      character raw between them, a line break among them.
      ::snippet~(::string | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::keyword | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:string/isAString
  |~~ Text in quotes is a string. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | type | eq ::string)

:string/buildsAString
  |~~ The constructor of a string answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(::string"x" | eq "x")

:string/readsTheEscapesOfJson
  |~~ A tab and a quote are written by their escapes. ~~|
  ::law~("A\t\"" | eq "A	\"")

:string/readsAUnicodeEscape
  |~~ A unicode escape writes its letter. ~~|
  ::law~("\u0041" | eq "A")

:keyword
  |~~ The kind of a keyword, `:k`, a name that stands for itself. It is
      apart from the string so that no name is read by its look: every
      key of a map is a keyword, `/k` reads a field by it, and a keyword
      names a binding of the scope; `keyword` makes one of a string and
      a string of one. A bare keyword is a name that begins with a letter
      of any script, `_` or `@` and goes on with letters, digits, `_` and
      `-`; a slash joins names into a path, `:domain/user`;
      any other text is written in quotes, `:"foo bar"`, and the quotes
      around a bare name change nothing. ::link~(::map | doc) ::link~(::binding | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:keyword/isAKeyword
  |~~ `:k` is a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(:k | type | eq ::keyword)

:keyword/buildsAKeyword
  |~~ The constructor of a keyword answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(::keyword:k | eq :k)

:keyword/takesThreeForms
  |~~ A name of any script, a name with a path and a quoted name are keywords alike. ~~|
  ::law~([:данные :domain/user :"$ref"] | map ~keyword | eq ["данные" "domain/user" "$ref"])

:keyword/quotesABareName
  |~~ A bare name quoted is the same keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(:"foo" | eq :foo)

:vec
  |~~ The kind of a vector, the value of a `[…]` literal, and the shape a
      long value takes: its parts have an index, a size and a slice,
      `drop` and `take`, so the rest of a long answer is the same query
      with a tail, and `map` runs a step on each element. A set, a quote
      and a doc are vectors under their tags, read as vectors wherever a
      vector is read; over them the constructor answers the vector of
      their elements, and `::vec[1 2]` reads as `[1 2]`. A JSON array is
      a vector literal too, a comma between every two elements; one literal
      is written in one of the two forms, as a map's is.
      ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::vec/map | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:vec/readsASetAsItsVector
  |~~ A set under `::vec` is its vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[2 1] | tag ::vec | eq [1 2])

:vec/takesCommas
  |~~ Commas between elements are spaces. ~~|
  ::law~("[1, 2]" | parse | apply / | eq [1 2])

:set
  |~~ The set, the vector in the one order without duplicates, the value
      of a `#[…]` literal, of `distinct` and of `keys`; `has` asks it
      for membership. A set is read as a vector wherever a vector is
      read; `filter`, `take`, `drop` and `flat` keep it, since its
      constructor re-runs after them, and a word that imposes an order,
      `sort` or `reverse`, answers a vector. `map` over a set answers
      the vector of its images, one per element, and `distinct` after it
      makes a set of them again. A set written with braces, `#{…}`, is
      refused with the brackets it takes.
      ::link~(::vec | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:set/dropsDuplicates
  |~~ A set literal drops duplicates. ~~|
  ::law~("#[3 1 3]" | parse | apply / | eq #[1 3])

:set/buildsFromAVector
  |~~ A vector under `::set` is its distinct elements. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 3] | tag ::set | eq ([3 1 3] | distinct))

:set/reversesIntoAVector
  |~~ A reversed set is a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 2] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])

:set/refusesBraces
  |~~ `#{1}` does not parse, and its message names `#[`. ~~|
  ::law~("#{1}" | parse !| /message | contains "#[")

:map
  |~~ The kind of a map, the value of a `{…}` literal: named facts under
      keywords, the shape a JSON object arrives in. A map literal is also
      the shape an answer takes, each value a group run on the subject,
      `{:id /id :n (/tags | count)}`; `/k` reads a field, and `map` over
      a map runs a step on each value under its key. A JSON object is a
      map literal too, its string keys read as keywords, so JSON pastes
      into a query as it is; one literal is written in one of the two
      forms, and a key written twice holds its last value. A map comes
      apart into its pairs `[key value]` by `entries` and back by
      `tag ::map`, which reads a key written as a string or a number as
      the keyword of its text.
      ::snippet~(::map | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals. ::link~(::proj | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:map/isAMap
  |~~ Braces make a map. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | type | eq ::map)

:map/buildsAMap
  |~~ The constructor of a map answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(::map{:a 1} | eq {:a 1})

:map/readsJson
  |~~ A JSON object is a map literal. ~~|
  ::law~("{\"a\": 1, \"b\": [true, null]}" | parse | apply / | eq {:a 1 :b [true null]})

:map/keepsTheLastOfAKey
  |~~ A key written twice keeps the last value. ~~|
  ::law~("{:a 1 :a 2}" | parse | apply / | eq {:a 2})

:map/buildsFromPairs
  |~~ A vector of pairs under `::map` is a map. ~~|
  ::law~([[:a 1] ["b" 2]] | tag ::map | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/buildsBackFromEntries
  |~~ The entries of a map build it back. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | entries | tag ::map | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:quote
  |~~ Code as a value, the value of a `~(…)` literal: a vector of steps
      under the code tag. Code is a value so that a name takes code as a
      part of its args, `filter`, `sort`, `if`; a page holds links that
      lead on and snippets that run, and the laws of a name are code; the
      trail of an error holds the steps it skipped, ready to replay; and
      a query is read, printed and built like any data. A literal step is
      the literal itself; a step that computes is a record, `::call`,
      `::proj`, `::bind` or `::construct`; `!|` and parentheses wrap their
      step as `::fail` and `::group`. `apply` runs a quote on the subject
      and `parse` prints it back.
      ::link~(::call | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/code | doc)
  ~~|

:quote/countsItsSteps
  |~~ A quote of three steps counts three. ~~|
  ::law~(~(1 | add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 3)

:quote/equalsItsCopy
  |~~ Two quotes of one code are equal. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1) | eq ~(add 1))

:quote/buildsBackFromItsSteps
  |~~ The steps of a quote build it back. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | payload | tag ::quote | eq ~(add 1 | mul 2))

:quote/filtersItsSteps
  |~~ A quote filters as the vector of its steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | filter ~(/name | eq :mul) | parse | eq "mul 2")

:doc
  |~~ The kind of a doc, prose as a value, the value of a doc literal:
      text that carries quotes of qlang. It is how the language says what
      it is: every page is a doc, `doc` answers the page of a name, and
      because a doc is a value its code is checked and the next step can
      take it apart. A quote under `::link` is a link, a query a reader
      follows to another page, one under `::snippet` a query a reader
      runs to see what it shows, and a quote with no tag is code the
      prose holds. `content` answers its text, `segments` its parts,
      `links` its links and `snippets` its snippets; the laws of a name
      stand under it.
      ::snippet~(::doc | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals. ::link~(::link | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)
  ~~|

:doc/isThePageOfAName
  |~~ The page of a name is a doc. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/add | doc | type | eq ::doc)

:doc/splitsIntoSegments
  |~~ A doc of prose, code and prose has three segments. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 3)

:doc/buildsFromSegments
  |~~ Segments under `::doc` build the doc. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" ~(add 1)] | tag ::doc | eq (|~~a~(add 1)~~|))

:binding
  |~~ The kind of the record a declaration writes into its scope. A name
      is a record that carries its page and its source, so what is named
      can be listed and asked: `:name`, the keyword it binds, `:docs`,
      one doc per doc of its slot, `:value`, `:source`, the quote of the
      declaring step, and `:origin`, the source or the cell it was read
      from. `env` answers the records of the scope, a name reads the value
      its record holds, and the axes project the record, so `env | /x |
      doc` answers the page of `x`. A value a host binds is a binding
      without a doc and without a source. ::link~(::bind | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)
  ~~|

:binding/holdsTheValue
  |~~ The record of a name holds its value. ~~|
  ::law~(:x |~~ One. ~~| 1 | env | /x | /value | eq 1)

:binding/holdsThePage
  |~~ The record of a name holds its page. ~~|
  ::law~(:x |~~ One. ~~| 1 | env | /x | doc | content | eq " One. ")

:binding/holdsTheName
  |~~ The record of a name holds the name. ~~|
  ::law~(:x 1 | env | /x | /name | eq :x)

:error
  |~~ The kind of errors. A reader learning a tool fails often, and
      a failure is the moment when the knowledge it needs is cheapest to
      give, so a failure is a value that carries it: the facts of the
      failure, the path it took, and the tag of the site that refused,
      which names the page of the procedure. An error is a map of facts
      under `::error`, `::error{:k 1}`, a value like any other until it
      is raised, and the bang of a literal raises it, `!{:k 1}`. Every
      refusal is a tag of its own, named for what failed,
      `::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError`, stacked over the error. `|` passes a
      raised error by, `map` stops on one, and `!|` opens it to that
      stack, so `!| type` reads the site, the verbs of this kind reach
      every error, and every step works on it as on any value: a fact is
      added and the error raised again, or it is recovered by the tag of
      its site. ::snippet~(::error | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::explanation | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|

:error/isAnError
  |~~ A failure literal opens to an `::error`. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:detail "boom"} !| type | eq ::error)

:error/tagsARefusalByItsSite
  |~~ A refusal's type is its site, not `::error`. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::error | not)

:error/standsOverError
  |~~ Beneath the site's tag lies an `::error`. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| payload | type | eq ::error)

:error/readsItsFacts
  |~~ An error's facts read as fields. ~~|
  ::law~(::error{:k 1} | /k | eq 1)

:error/takesAFactOnTheWay
  |~~ A fact added on the fail track rides on. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| union {:request "r-1"} | raise !| /request | eq "r-1")

:error/recoversByItsType
  |~~ A failure of a known type recovers. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError) ~(0) ~(raise) | eq 0)

:error/raisesAgainOtherwise
  |~~ A failure of another type raises again as itself. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::Other) ~(0) ~(raise) !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)

:elision
  |~~ The kind of the marker of what a view left out. A
      reader's context only grows, and whatever an answer adds is read
      again on every later step, so a reader asks for a budget, by
      `elide` in the query or by the host's flag, and the marker keeps
      the way back; a value nobody bounded prints whole, and a failure
      as an alert, each subject it passed a marker: `:size` counts the elements, the
      entries or the characters it stands for, `:read` is the quote that
      applied to the value taken answers the part left out, and `:head`
      holds the first characters of a string. `elide` writes markers,
      and a reader continues its query by `:read` to see the rest.
      ::link~(::any/elide | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc)
  ~~|

:elision/hasAPage
  |~~ The kind has a page. ~~|
  ::law~(::elision | doc | type | eq ::doc)

:elision/measuresWhatItLeftOut
  |~~ An elision holds the size of what it left out. ~~|
  ::law~({:s "a long line of text to leave out"} | elide 20 | /s | /size | eq 32)

:desc
  |~~ The kind of a value turned to descend: among the values of this
      kind the one order runs in reverse, the greater payload first, so a
      key of `sort`, `min` or `max` under `desc` descends whatever the
      kind of what it ranks, a string among them, and a place of a vector
      key descends alone, `sort ~([(/count | desc) /name])`. Every
      comparison agrees, `gt` and `lt` here as `sort` and `max`, and
      `payload` opens the value beneath. `desc` lays the kind over any
      value. ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:desc/holdsTheValue
  |~~ The payload is the value turned. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | desc | payload | eq 5)

:desc/buildsByItsConstructor
  |~~ The constructor turns as `desc` does. ~~|
  ::law~(::desc(5) | eq (5 | desc))

:desc/sortsInReverse
  |~~ Turned values sort in reverse. ~~|
  ::law~([2 1 3] | map ~desc | sort | map ~payload | eq [3 2 1])

:desc/descendsAKey
  |~~ A key of `desc` sorts in reverse. ~~|
  ::law~(["b" "c" "a"] | sort ~(desc) | eq ["c" "b" "a"])

:link
  |~~ The kind of a link, a query a page names for its reader to follow,
      written in the prose of a doc as a quote under `::link`.
      Links are what make the pages a hypertext: a page names the pages
      that go deeper, and a reader reaches them by `open`, which runs the
      query the link holds. A link opens a page, so `links | map ~open`
      answers pages alone, and a query that lists or shows something is a
      snippet; a law answers true, and stands under the name it proves. The suite
      follows every link of the catalog, so none goes dead. A host may
      stack its own tag over a link.
      ::snippet~(::qlang | doc | links) lists the links of the first page.
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)
  ~~|

:link/isALink
  |~~ A link a doc holds is a `::link`. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ ::link~(::qlang/values | doc) ~~| | links | first | type | eq ::link)

:link/opensToPages
  |~~ The links of the first page open to pages. ~~|
  ::law~(::qlang | doc | links | map ~open | every ~(type | eq ::doc))

:link/holdsUnderAStackOfTags
  |~~ A quote under a stack of tags holding `::link` is a link. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ ::Host::link~(1) ~~| | links | count | eq 1)

:snippet
  |~~ The kind of a snippet, a query a page shows its reader to run and
      see, written in the prose of a doc as a quote under `::snippet`: a
      listing, ::snippet~(::vec | manifest), or a demonstration of
      a form, ::snippet~({:a [5 6]} | /a/-1). A law answers true and a
      link opens a page; a snippet answers whatever it shows, which
      `open` runs, and `snippets` reads the snippets of a doc. A snippet
      reproduces a situation for its reader, so it may answer an error;
      the suite holds it to reading as code alone.
      ::link~(::link | doc) ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)
  ~~|

:snippet/opensToWhatItShows
  |~~ A snippet opens to what it shows. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) ~~| | snippets | first | open | has ::vec/count)

:snippet/showsInThePageOfVectors
  |~~ The page of vectors shows a snippet. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | doc | snippets | count | gt 0)

:law
  |~~ The kind of a law, a query that answers true, declared as a
      definition under the name whose behaviour it proves: the name says
      the claim, the page is its sentence, and `laws` answers the laws
      of a name, which `runLaws` runs.
  ~~|

:law/provesItsClaim
  |~~ A law declared under a name proves its claim when it runs. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/k ~(5) | :any/k/answersFive |~~ k answers five. ~~| ::law~(0 | k | eq 5) | ::any/k | runLaws | map ~/ok | eq [true])

:holds
  |~~ The kind of an invariant, a query that the values of a label, the
      args of a word, a part of them or the answer of a word keeps,
      declared as a definition under the name it holds and named by the
      refusal of a value that breaks it, `:Small/TooLargeError ::holds~(lt
      10)`: the value is checked when it is built, read or answered, and
      refused under that name when the query answers false.
  ~~|

:holds/refusesUnderItsName
  |~~ A value that breaks an invariant is refused under its name. ~~|
  ::law~(:Small |~~ Below ten. ~~| | :Small/TooLargeError ::holds~(lt 10) | [(::Small(3) | type) (::Small(12) !| type)] | eq [::Small ::Small/TooLargeError])

:explanation
  |~~ The kind of what `explain` answers: the page `doc` reads
      for a value under `:page`, then the value itself under `:value`, so
      it prints as the page above what it explains, an error
      raised, with its bang. It is the cord from a value to its
      procedure in one answer; `page` and `value` answer its parts.
      ::link~(::error | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|

:explanation/explainsAnError
  |~~ An error explained is an `::explanation`. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| explain | type | eq ::explanation)

:tagged
  |~~ The kind of every value under a tag, `::Box[1 2]`, `::Box{:a 1}`,
      whatever the payload: the walk from a tagged value to its verb
      passes its own tags, then this kind, then the kind of its payload's
      shape. The verbs that take a tag apart live here, `payload`, which
      answers the value beneath, and `within`, which edits it and puts
      the tag back through its constructor. A value is put under a tag
      by a literal, `::T[1 2]` or `::T{:a 1}`, or by `tag`; the literal
      in a quote is a step of its own kind, `::construct`, and no value of
      this one. Every value under a tag reaches the words of this kind.
      ::link~(::tag | doc) ::link~(::construct | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:tagged/reachesPayload
  |~~ A box answers `payload`. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[1 2] | payload | eq [1 2])

:tagged/isNoStep
  |~~ The step `::Box[1 2]` is a `::construct`. ~~|
  ::law~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | type | eq ::construct)

:construct
  |~~ The step of a constructor invocation, `::T[…]`, `::T{…}`, `::T(…)`
      or `::T"…"`: it puts a payload under the tag `::T` through the tag's
      constructor, which checks or builds the value. As a record, `:tag`
      is the tag and `:payload` the step of its payload. ::link~(::tag | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:construct/holdsTheTagAndThePayload
  |~~ The step holds its tag and its payload. ~~|
  ::law~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | [/tag /payload] | eq [::Box [1 2]])

:call
  |~~ The step of a command, `verb modifiers`: the name finds its verb
      through the kind of the subject and runs it with the modifiers.
      As a record, `:name` is the keyword of the name it calls and
      `:modifiers` the step of each modifier, when the call gives any.
      Like every record, a `::call` is the step its own text reads back
      as: the constructor checks the fields against the schema, prints
      the step and reads it back. ::link~(::quote | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:call/holdsTheNameAndTheModifiers
  |~~ A command is a call of its name and its modifiers. ~~|
  ::law~(~(filter ~(gt 1)) | payload | eq [::call{:name :filter :modifiers [~(gt 1)]}])

:call/readsTheName
  |~~ The name of the call is a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(~(count) | first | /name | eq :count)

:call/buildsAStep
  |~~ A map under `::call` builds the step. ~~|
  ::law~({:name :filter :modifiers [~(gt 1)]} | tag ::call | [/] | tag ::quote | eq ~(filter ~(gt 1)))

:proj
  |~~ The step of a projection, `/a/0/b`: it reads a part of the value in
      the pipe by keys and indices, a negative index from the end, and a
      miss answers an error, never null, so a misspelled field is found
      where it is read. `/` is the value itself. A key is written as a
      keyword is, bare, in quotes, `/"foo bar"`, or with its colon when it
      is a path, `/:qlang/error`, which is one key where `/qlang/error`
      is two. As a record, `:path` is the keys and indices it walks,
      `[]` for `/`.
      ::snippet~({:a {:b [5 6]}} | /a/b/-1) answers `6`.
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:proj/holdsThePath
  |~~ A projection holds its path of keys and indices. ~~|
  ::law~(~(/items/0/name) | first | /path | eq [:items 0 :name])

:proj/holdsTheEmptyPath
  |~~ `/` alone has the empty path. ~~|
  ::law~(~(/) | first | /path | eq [])

:proj/readsQuotedAndPathKeys
  |~~ Quoted keys and keys with a path read in a path. ~~|
  ::law~({:"foo bar" {:qlang/error 1}} | /"foo bar"/:qlang/error | eq 1)

:proj/readsNestedKeys
  |~~ A path of two keys reads nested maps. ~~|
  ::law~({:qlang {:error 2}} | /qlang/error | eq 2)

:proj/readsTheWholeValue
  |~~ `/` reads the whole value. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | mul / | eq 25)

:bind
  |~~ The step of a declaration, `:name body`: it names a value for the
      steps after it, in the scope where it stands, and a group, an
      element of a literal and a quote a word runs each open a scope of
      their own, so a name never leaks out of the fork that declared it. As a
      record, `:name` is the keyword or tag it binds, `:docs` its prose
      and `:body` the step of its body. ::link~(::binding | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:bind/holdsTheNameAndTheBody
  |~~ A declaration holds its name and its body. ~~|
  ::law~(~(:inc add 1) | first | [/name /body/name] | eq [:inc :add])

:fail
  |~~ The step `!| step`, the fail track: it runs its step on an error
      alone, opened to its facts, and passes any other value by, so it is
      where a failure is read, recovered or raised again, while `|`
      passes the error by. As a record it wraps the quote of that one
      step. ::link~(::error | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|

:fail/holdsItsStep
  |~~ The fail track holds its step as a quote. ~~|
  ::law~(~(!| /kind) | first | payload | eq ~(/kind))

:group
  |~~ The step `(pipeline)`, a group: it runs its steps on the value in
      the pipe and answers their result, its names left inside. Each part
      of a literal is such a group, which is why a literal is the shape an
      answer takes, `[(add 1) (mul 2)]`. As a record it wraps the quote
      of its pipeline. ::link~(::bind | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:group/holdsItsSteps
  |~~ A group holds its steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~((add 1 | mul 2)) | first | payload | count | eq 2)

:any
  |~~ The kind beneath every kind, where the verbs every value answers
      live: `type`, `eq`, `doc`, `print`, `explain`, `elide` and
      their kin. A verb that means the same for any value is written once
      here, and the walk from a name to its verb ends here, after the
      kinds of the subject. No value answers it as its `type`, since each
      has a kind of its own above it.
      ::snippet~(::any | manifest) lists the verbs of every value.
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)
  ~~|

:any/holdsType
  |~~ `type` lives on `::any`. ~~|
  ::law~(::any | manifest | has ::any/type)

:any/isTheKindOfNoValue
  |~~ No value's type is `::any`. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | type | eq ::any | not)

:qlang/values
  |~~ Values. Everything qlang handles is a value, data, code, prose and
      failures alike, so everything the language says about itself is a
      value the pipeline reads. A value never changes, and each has a
      kind, the name of which is a tag: it tells how to read the value it
      stands on, and the tag taken as a value, `::Box`, is the kind
      itself, which a query asks for its page and its verbs. Rests on
      nothing; the pipeline, the verbs and the hypertext rest on it.
      ::snippet~(kinds) lists the kinds the scope reads.
      ::link~(::tag | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)

      A literal implies its kind.
      A tag written before a payload puts it under the tag, whatever
      brackets the payload takes, `::T{…}`, `::T[…]`, `::T(…)` or `::T"…"`.
      `tag` puts the value in the pipe under a tag, tags stack and read
      from the outside in, and `payload` takes one off.
      A step answers a new value and the old one stays as it was.
      Every value prints as a literal that reads back as itself.
  ~~|
  #[::tag ::null ::boolean ::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set]

:qlang/values/impliesTheKinds
  |~~ Each literal answers its kind. ~~|
  ::law~([42 "s" :k [1] {:k 1} #[1] true null] | map ~type | eq [::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set ::boolean ::null])

:qlang/values/putsAnyPayloadUnderATag
  |~~ Any brackets put a payload under a tag. ~~|
  ::law~([::Box{:a 1} ::Box[1 2] ::Box(1 | add 2) ::Box"s"] | map ~payload | eq [{:a 1} [1 2] 3 "s"])

:qlang/values/stacksTags
  |~~ Tags stack and read from the outside in. ~~|
  ::law~([7 8] | tag ::Inner | tag ::Outer | [type (payload | type) (payload | payload)] | eq [::Outer ::Inner [7 8]])

:qlang/values/readsTheOuterTag
  |~~ The type is the outer tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type | eq ::Box)

:qlang/values/changesNothingInPlace
  |~~ Appending leaves the old vector as it was. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | :v / | append 3 | [v /] | eq [[1 2] [1 2 3]])

:qlang/values/readsBackItsPrint
  |~~ A value reads back from its print. ~~|
  ::law~({:a [1 #[2]] :t ::Box[3]} | :v / | print | parse | :q / | apply q | eq v)

:qlang/pipeline
  |~~ The pipeline. A query is a subject and its steps, each a command
      on the value the step before it answered, and the answer is the
      last value; the shape of an answer is written as the literal it
      takes, so a query names what it reads. Rests on values; the verbs
      are its commands, and code holds its steps as a value.
      ::link~(::proj | doc) ::link~(::group | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/map | doc) ::link~(::qlang/names | doc)

      `|` runs the next step on the answer of the one before.
      A literal is a step that replaces the value in the pipe, a tag name
      among them, so a value goes under a tag by `tag`.
      `map` runs a quote on each element of a vector or each value of a
      map, a literal among its steps.
      A pipe written before `!|` stands for it.
      `/k`, `/0`, `/-1`, `/a/b` read a part, and a miss is an error.
      A modifier is computed against the subject of its own step, the
      value the step before it answered, so two fields of one record are
      read in one step, and a name keeps the record for a later step.
      A name holds the value in the pipe for the steps after it, and
      steps in parentheses run on the value in the pipe and answer their
      result.
      At the top level a line is a step, joined to the next by `|` unless
      one of them carries its combinator, and inside brackets a line
      break is a space; a comment, `|~ … ~|`, nesting, or `|~|` to the
      end of its line, is whitespace wherever a space stands.
      `|` and `!|` bind alike, from left to right. Each part of a
      literal and a group is a fork: it starts from the
      value and the names outside it, and only its answer leaves it, so
      the parts of one literal see nothing of each other.
  ~~|
  #[::proj ::group ::bind ::call ::construct]

:qlang/pipeline/runsStepAfterStep
  |~~ Five plus one, times two, is twelve. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | add 1 | mul 2 | eq 12)

:qlang/pipeline/replacesByALiteral
  |~~ A literal replaces the value in the pipe. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | [1 2] | eq [1 2])

:qlang/pipeline/replacesByATagName
  |~~ A tag name replaces it too. ~~|
  ::law~([7 8] | ::Box | eq ::Box)

:qlang/pipeline/takesAPipeBeforeTheFailTrack
  |~~ A pipe before `!|` stands for it. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 | !| 0 | eq 0)

:qlang/pipeline/readsAPath
  |~~ A path reads a nested part. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:b 1}} | /a/b | eq 1)

:qlang/pipeline/failsOnAMiss
  |~~ A miss is an error. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | /b !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:qlang/pipeline/keepsTheRecordByAName
  |~~ A name keeps the record for a later step. ~~|
  ::law~({:qty 2 :price 3} | :r / | /qty | mul (r | /price) | eq 6)

:qlang/pipeline/holdsTheValueByAName
  |~~ A name holds the value in the pipe. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | :n / | [n (n | add 1)] | eq [5 6])

:qlang/pipeline/joinsLines
  |~~ Lines join as steps, a comment between them. ~~|
  ::law~("[3 1 2]\n|~| the order\nsort\n| first" | parse | apply / | eq 1)

:qlang/pipeline/nestsComments
  |~~ Comments nest and stand where spaces stand. ~~|
  ::law~("|~ outer |~ inner ~| ~| [1 2] |~ a note ~| | count" | parse | apply / | eq 2)

:qlang/pipeline/seesNothingOfASibling
  |~~ One part of a literal sees nothing of another. ~~|
  ::law~([(:a 1 | a) (a)] | /1 !| type | eq ::call/UnknownNameError)

:qlang/names
  |~~ Names. A name holds a value for the steps after it, the one way a
      query remembers: `:n v` declares it in the scope where it stands,
      and a verb and a kind are names alike. A name with a path declares
      a node under the noun its path names before its last name,
      `:Box/area` under `::Box`, a noun declared before it, and the tag
      of a name is its address, `::Box/area`. A name is a record,
      `::binding`, with its page, its source, its value and the source
      it came from, so where a thing was declared is read as data. The
      scope of a step is what the steps before it wrote; a step inside
      `(…)` or a quote a word runs writes a scope that closes with it. Under
      every scope lies the root, which the host's sources declared as
      cells before the first step of a reader, the language's first; a
      query, a cell of a session and a source of the root are written
      alike. Rests on values and the pipeline; the verbs are names that
      run. ::link~(::binding | doc) ::link~(::bind | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)

      `env` lists what the reader declared, `kinds` the kinds the scope
      reads, and what lies under a noun `manifest` lists.
      A name reads its record by `binding`.
      A name declared inside a group closes with it.
      A name declared under a label resides on it: it serves the values
      of the label, found by its name through the walk of their tags,
      and refuses any other subject under its address; a quote taken
      from one label and declared under another resides on the second.
      A name is declared once in a scope and again in a scope inside it,
      which shadows it for the steps after; a value the reader names
      takes no word of the vocabulary, the name of a verb or of a name
      under a label, so no step meets it where the subject's word was
      meant; an address the root holds is declared once at all, so a
      reader extends a noun of the root and replaces none of its nodes.
      A name begins with a letter of any script, `_` or `@`; `@` marks a
      name whose call reaches outside the language, and a name whose body
      calls one carries it too, while a name over the value a call
      answered is plain.
      The docs in the slot of a declaration, between its name and its
      body, are its page, one doc each.
      A name under a label resolves its names through the tree of names
      as it stands at the call, itself among them, so it recurses by
      name, and the frames of a runaway meet a budget.
  ~~|
  #[::binding]

:qlang/names/listsUnderANoun
  |~~ `manifest` lists what lies under a noun. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | manifest | has ::vec/count)

:qlang/names/closesWithItsGroup
  |~~ A name declared in a group closes with it. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | (:inner 2 | inner) | env | has :inner | not)

:qlang/names/servesTheValuesOfItsLabel
  |~~ A name under a label serves its values. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/twice ~(/w | mul 2) | ::Box{:w 3} | twice | eq 6)

:qlang/names/extendsANounOfTheLanguage
  |~~ A reader's name under `vec` serves vectors. ~~|
  ::law~(:vec/second ~(/1) | [7 8 9] | second | eq 8)

:qlang/names/refusesAnotherSubject
  |~~ Another subject is refused under the name's address. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/w2 ~(/w | mul 2) | {:w 3} | Box/w2 !| type | eq ::Box/w2/SubjectNotBoxError)

:qlang/names/residesWhereDeclared
  |~~ A quote taken from one label resides on the one it is declared under. ~~|
  ::law~(:A |~~ A. ~~| | :A/f ~(1) | :B |~~ B. ~~| | :B/g (::A/f | binding | /value) | [(::B{} | g) (::A{} | B/g !| type)] | eq [1 ::B/g/SubjectNotBError])

:qlang/names/needsItsNoun
  |~~ A name under no declared noun is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box/area ~(1) !| type | eq ::bind/NounNotDeclaredError)

:qlang/names/declaresOnce
  |~~ A name is declared once in a scope. ~~|
  ::law~(:k 1 | :k 2 !| type | eq ::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError)

:qlang/names/takesNoWord
  |~~ A value takes no word of the vocabulary. ~~|
  ::law~(:count 5 !| type | eq ::bind/NameTakenByVocabularyError)

:qlang/names/leavesAValuePlain
  |~~ A name over the value a call answered is plain. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/@io ~(1) | :fine (@io) | fine | eq 1)

:qlang/names/marksAWordThatReachesOut
  |~~ A word that calls a marked name carries the mark. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/quiet ~(@out) !| type | eq ::bind/EffectMarkMissingError)

:qlang/names/holdsEachDocOfItsSlot
  |~~ Two docs in the slot are two parts of its page. ~~|
  ::law~(:x |~~ One. ~~| |~~ Two. ~~| 1 | env | /x | /docs | count | eq 2)

:qlang/names/recursesByName
  |~~ A name calls itself through the tree of names. ~~|
  ::law~(:map/walk ~({:n /n :kids (/kids | map ~walk)}) | {:n 1 :kids [{:n 2 :kids []}]} | walk | /kids/0/n | eq 2)

:qlang/names/meetsABudget
  |~~ A runaway meets the budget of frames. ~~|
  ::law~(:number/inf ~(add 1 | inf) | 0 | inf !| type | eq ::call/EvaluationDepthExceededError)

:qlang/verbs
  |~~ Verbs. A verb lives on a kind, the one whose values it serves, and
      has an address, `::vec/sort`; so what can be done with a value is
      asked of its kind, and one word serves many kinds. The verbs of a
      kind and those of every value are the whole of them: a verb in
      neither list does not exist. Rests on values and the pipeline;
      errors name the verb that refused.
      ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) lists the verbs of a kind and
      ::snippet~(::any | manifest) those of every value.
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)

      A name finds its verb through the kind of its subject: its tags
      from the outside in, then the shape beneath them, then the verbs
      every value answers, on `::any`; a verb of vectors reaches a
      vector under a tag and keeps the tag.
      A name declares the parts of its args as names under it, each
      kind's word its own, and the label its answer goes under; `manifest`
      answers them before a call.
      An answer of the word's own label goes back under the tags the
      subject came through.
      A reader's name under a noun takes the parts of its args after
      it, each a name under the call's address.
      A modifier is evaluated at the call against the subject, the value
      in the pipe, which is always the subject of the call; a form that
      combines several values of one subject is a verb of its own, which
      reads each from its args, `mulOf /price /qty`; a part that runs code
      later takes a quote, `~name` or `~/path` the quote of one step, and
      a condition is a boolean.
      A kind is a label, a name declared by its page, whose invariants
      every value put under it keeps, and whose name `construct`, when it has
      one, builds the value from the payload.
      A tag no declaration answers is a kind of identity, which checks
      nothing and lays the tag on the value.
      A word answers under the label its `answer` names.
      A word of a noun itself, whose `subject` names `::tag`, takes the
      tag name as its subject, so a guardian with no payload is addressed
      bare, `::Jenkins | builds`, while a word of the noun's values
      refuses the tag name.
  ~~|
  #[::any]

:qlang/verbs/findsTheVerbOfTheKind
  |~~ `sort` finds the verb of vectors. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 2] | sort | eq [1 2 3])

:qlang/verbs/keepsTheTag
  |~~ A verb of vectors reaches a vector under a tag and keeps the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[3 1 2] | sort | eq ::Box[1 2 3])

:qlang/verbs/declaresTheParts
  |~~ `at` declares its answer, its key and its default. ~~|
  ::law~(::map/at | manifest | eq #[::map/at/answer ::map/at/default ::map/at/key])

:qlang/verbs/answersUnderTheTags
  |~~ A sum goes back under the tag the subject came through. ~~|
  ::law~(::Width(2) | add 3 | eq ::Width(5))

:qlang/verbs/takesAReadersPart
  |~~ A reader's word reads its part of the args. ~~|
  ::law~(:number/plus ~(add by) | :number/plus/by |~~ How much. ~~| ~(/0) | :number/plus/by/NotNumberError ::holds~(is ::number) | 5 | plus 2 | eq 7)

:qlang/verbs/combinesValuesOfOneSubject
  |~~ A word that combines several values of one subject reads each from its args. ~~|
  ::law~({:price 100 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 300)

:qlang/verbs/takesAQuoteForLaterCode
  |~~ Code run later is a quote. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(/age | gt 18) | count | eq 1)

:qlang/verbs/quotesOneStep
  |~~ `~/age` quotes one step. ~~|
  ::law~("[{:age 3}] | sort ~/age | first | /age" | parse | apply / | eq 3)

:qlang/verbs/needsABoolean
  |~~ A condition is a boolean. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | filter ~(1) !| type | eq ::vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError)

:qlang/verbs/laysAnUndeclaredTag
  |~~ A tag no declaration answers checks nothing. ~~|
  ::law~(::Small(12) | payload | eq 12)

:qlang/verbs/answersUnderItsAnswer
  |~~ A word answers under the label its `answer` names. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/half ~(/0 | div 2) | :Box/half/answer |~~ The half. ~~| ::number | ::Box[4] | half | eq 2)

:qlang/verbs/takesTheTagOfItsNoun
  |~~ A word of a noun itself takes the tag name as its subject. ~~|
  ::law~(:Desk |~~ A help desk. ~~| | :Desk/hours ~("9 to 5") | :Desk/hours/subject |~~ The desk itself. ~~| ::tag | ::Desk | hours | eq "9 to 5")

:qlang/verbs/refusesTheTagForAWordOfValues
  |~~ A word of the noun's values refuses the tag name. ~~|
  ::law~(:Desk |~~ A help desk. ~~| | :Desk/size ~(count) | ::Desk | size !| type | eq ::Desk/size/SubjectNotDeskError)

:qlang/errors
  |~~ Errors. A failure does not throw: a step that fails answers an
      error, a value that carries the facts of the failure, the path it
      took and the tag of the site that refused, which names the page of
      its procedure. A reader learning a tool fails often, and the
      failure is where what it needs is cheapest to give. Rests on
      values, the pipeline and verbs; the hypertext holds the pages the
      sites name.
      ::link~(::error | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::explanation | doc)

      A failed step answers an error the tag of its site names.
      `|` passes an error by, and `map` answers the first an element
      answers.
      `a !| b` runs `b` on an error alone, opened to its facts, and
      recovers each failure where it is read.
      The facts name what refused, and the first stop of the trail holds
      the value the step refused.
      `explain` shows the page of the site above the error.
      The trail holds a stop for each level the error left, and the last
      stop the steps it skipped, a quote ready to replay.
      A map raised again after `!|` resumes the trail it holds, and one
      without `:trail` starts it over.
      Whether a value is an error is `false !| true`.
  ~~|
  #[::error ::fail ::explanation]

:qlang/errors/namesTheSite
  |~~ The error's tag names its site. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)

:qlang/errors/recoversWhereRead
  |~~ `!|` inside the code `map` runs recovers each failure. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2 !| 0) | eq [2 0])

:qlang/errors/holdsTheRefusedValue
  |~~ The first stop holds the refused value. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | mul 2 !| [/actualType /trail/0/subject] | eq [::string "a"])

:qlang/errors/stopsAtEachLevel
  |~~ The trail stops at each level the error left. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/f ~(add n | mul n) | :any/f/n ~(/0) | "x" | f 2 | sub 3 !| /trail | map ~/step | eq [~(add n) ~(f 2)])

:qlang/errors/holdsTheSkippedSteps
  |~~ The last stop holds the steps skipped. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| /trail/-1/skipped | parse | eq "mul 2")

:qlang/errors/resumesItsTrail
  |~~ A map raised again resumes its trail. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:kind :oops} | count !| raise | add 1 !| /trail | map ~/step | eq [~(!{:kind :oops})])

:qlang/errors/asksWhetherItFailed
  |~~ `false !| true` answers whether a value is an error. ~~|
  ::law~([!{:kind :oops} 42] | map ~(false !| true) | eq [true false])

:qlang/code
  |~~ Code. A quote, `~(…)`, holds steps unrun as a value: a verb takes
      one where it needs code, `filter`, `sort`, `if`, `apply` runs one on
      a subject, and a quote is taken apart and built like any vector of
      steps. Because code is a value, the laws of a name run, a page
      holds links that open, and the trail of an error holds the steps it
      skipped, ready to replay. Rests on values and the pipeline; the
      hypertext is written in it.
      ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)
  ~~|
  #[::quote]

:qlang/code/runsByApply
  |~~ A quote held as a value runs by `apply`. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | :q / | 5 | apply q | eq 12)

:qlang/code/isAVectorOfSteps
  |~~ A quote counts its steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 2)

:qlang/hypertext
  |~~ The hypertext. The names are a space of addresses, `::` beginning
      an absolute one, `::vec/sort`, and a verb passes from an address
      into one of its projections, as a path reaches a file: `doc` into
      its page, `binding` into its record, `source` into the code
      written under it, and `runLaws` runs the laws under it. An address holds no one
      entity but a set of values, and each passage reads one of them; the
      address, the verb and what it reaches are all values, so the
      pipeline goes on from a projection as from any value, and a tag
      you declare with verbs on it adds projections of its own.
      A keyword after `doc` is an anchor within the page of its subject,
      `::qlang/pipeline | doc`, a vector of anchors a path, a set of
      anchors several pages at once. A page is a doc, prose that holds
      code: a quote under `::link` is a link to another page, and a
      quote under `::snippet` a query a reader runs to see what it
      shows, `open` running either. So a page leads on to the pages that
      go deeper, and the pipeline moves through them as through any data:
      `manifest` lists what lies under a noun, its verbs and refusals,
      `links | map ~open` opens every page a page names in one query, and a
      filter over pages by `content | contains` searches them. Nothing
      is read that was not asked for, and a query names the shape of
      what it reads. The laws that prove a name stand under it, which
      `runLaws` holds to answer true. Rests on every concept before it.
      ::link~(::doc | doc) ::link~(::link | doc) ::link~(::snippet | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc)
  ~~|
  #[::doc ::link ::snippet]

:qlang/hypertext/opensTheLinksOfAPage
  |~~ The links of a page open in one query. ~~|
  ::law~(::qlang | doc | links | map ~open | count | gt 0)

:qlang/hypertext/passesIntoThreeAxes
  |~~ One address passes into its page, its record and its source. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take | [(doc | type) (binding | type) (source | type)] | eq [::doc ::binding ::quote])

:qlang/hypertext/searchesThePages
  |~~ A filter over pages finds `drop`. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | manifest | filter ~(doc | content | contains "after the first") | eq #[::vec/drop])

:qlang/hosts
  |~~ Hosts. The language runs inside a host, a tool that embeds it and
      brings verbs of its own; the command line is one. A value is whole
      in the language, and the host prints a value whole unless a
      reader asks for a budget, by `elide` in the query or by the host's
      flag, the command line's `--budget=N`: an answer whose print fits
      the budget stays whole, and what a budget left out is an
      `::elision` marker, whose `:read` continues the query to the part.
      A failure the host prints is an alert: its tag and its facts, the
      subject where it arose within an excerpt, and a marker for each
      subject it passed, the failure itself staying whole.
      The command line reads JSON or text from stdin and writes
      its answer back in the same form. Rests on values and the hypertext.
      ::link~(::elision | doc) ::link~(::any/elide | doc)
  ~~|
  #[::elision]
